1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,805
Japan's bullet trains can begin

2
00:00:01,805 --> 00:00:03,875
braking before strong earthquake shaking

3
00:00:03,875 --> 00:00:05,250
arrives.

4
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:07,070
This is early warning, not

5
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:09,855
prediction. The earthquake has already

6
00:00:09,855 --> 00:00:11,100
started.

7
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,765
J R East's system detects

8
00:00:12,765 --> 00:00:14,685
fast P waves, the first

9
00:00:14,685 --> 00:00:16,770
seismic vibrations.

10
00:00:16,870 --> 00:00:18,505
Slower S waves and surface

11
00:00:18,505 --> 00:00:21,405
waves generally bring stronger shaking.

12
00:00:21,505 --> 00:00:22,755
Imagine a sensor near the

13
00:00:22,755 --> 00:00:25,305
earthquake. It detects the first

14
00:00:25,305 --> 00:00:27,055
wave, then sends a warning

15
00:00:27,055 --> 00:00:28,735
toward the railway.

16
00:00:28,835 --> 00:00:30,480
When the railway system decides

17
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,160
action is needed, it cuts

18
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,555
power, triggering the train's emergency

19
00:00:34,555 --> 00:00:35,890
brakes.

20
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:38,120
The electrical signal outruns seismic

21
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:40,620
waves. That means braking can

22
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:42,305
start while stronger shaking is

23
00:00:42,305 --> 00:00:43,935
still on its way.

24
00:00:44,035 --> 00:00:45,665
But near the earthquake, the

25
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:47,995
warning may arrive too late.

26
00:00:47,995 --> 00:00:49,730
Braking early does not guarantee

27
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:51,820
stopping before shaking.

28
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:53,770
The takeaway: detect what has

29
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:55,480
started, then use the time

30
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:57,665
difference to reduce risk.
